<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>16: General Fourier series</title>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the Fourier sine series for the function <m>f(x) = x</m> over
  the interval <m>[0,1]</m>.  Plot an approximation to your series
  along with the function <m>f(x)</m> over this interval.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now find the Fourier cosine series for this function over the same
  interval.  Plot an approximation to your series
  along with the function <m>f(x)</m> over this interval.</p>
        <p>You should see the Gibbs phenomenon in one case but not the other.
  We'd like to understand why this happens.  Let's look at another
  example.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the general Fourier series for the function <m>f(x) = x^2 + x
  + 1</m> over the interval <m>[-1,1]</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use Mathematica to graph the function <m>f(x)</m> and an approximation to
  the Fourier series over the interval <m>[-1,1]</m>.  No matter how many
  terms you add, your Fourier series should exhibit the ``Gibbs
  phenomenon,'' which shows a lot of oscillations near the endpoints.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now graph just the Fourier series over the interval <m>[-4,4]</m>.  Here
  you should see the periodicity in the Fourier series and the reason
  for the Gibbs phenomenon as the Fourier series is trying to track
  a discontinuity.</p>
        <p>We'll discuss this as a class in a little while.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
